    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a 12 week hypertrophy block for a client but they've hit a serious plateau on their back squat. they haven't added any weight in 3 weeks and their RPE is hitting 10 way too early. can you explain how deload weeks and changing up the volume might help them break through this? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Considering your client is plateaued on back squats, deload weeks can be a helpful strategy to allow their bodies to recover and reset. Reducing the overall volume, intensity, and frequency of training, specifically for the back squat, for 1-2 weeks can help their nervous system and muscles recover, allowing for a more gradual adaptation. This can also help them relearn proper form and technique, which might be contributing to the plateau. By dropping the RPE to a level where it feels more manageable, they can continue making progress without feeling maximal overload, allowing for a more sustainable progress.",
