    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if we do a deload, how much should we actually drop the intensity at? i'm thinking of maybe cutting the working sets in half or just dropping the load by like 20%? also, how does volume manipulation specifically help with the physiological side of the plateau—like, is it more about metabolic stress or actual muscle fiber recruitment?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When implementing a deload, reducing the load by 20% is a reasonable approach. This will give the nervous system and muscles a chance to recover while still maintaining some tension, and can help reduce the RPE to a more manageable level. You can also consider reducing the frequency of back squat training, or dropping the intensity with lower volumes of 3-4 sets instead of the usual 4-6 sets.\n\nVolume manipulation helps with the physiological side of the plateau by allowing the body to recover and adapt to the training stimulus. When you reduce the overall volume, you're reducing the metabolic stress, which can lead to increased recovery time and decreased muscle damage. This can actually allow for more potent fiber recruitment and cellular adaptations in subsequent deload phases. Think of it as giving your body a \"refuel\" period before you ramp back up to the heavy lifting.",
      "source": "target",
