    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'm also curious about the CNS side of it. if they're hitting RPE 10 every session, it feels like their central nervous system is just fried. can you go a bit deeper into the neurological aspect of why the plateau happens? like, why does the brain basically stop letting them push harder even if the muscles aren't totally trashed?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When an individual reaches an RPE of 10, their central nervous system (CNS) is indeed fatigued, and this can have a profound impact on their ability to-produce power and force. When the CNS becomes fatigued, it can lead to changes in neuronal excitability, neurotransmitter release, and muscle prime contracting patterns. One way to understand this is by considering the concepts of \"neural drive\" and \"recruitment threshold\".\n\nWhen the CNS is fatigued, the recruitment threshold (the point at which the motor neuron fires and contracts a muscle fiber) becomes elevated, making it more difficult to recruit higher-power muscle fibers, such as fast-twitch (FT) fibers. At the same time, the discharge pattern of motor neurons, which corresponds to the temporal activation of muscle fibers, becomes more inefficient, leading to more incomplete contractions and energy waste.\n\nAs a result, when an individual reaches an RPE of 10, the CNS is essentially saying, \"Okay, we've had enough of this heavy lifting. Let's conserve energy by reprogramming the motor neurons and making it more difficult to produce power and force.\" This can lead to plateaus, as the CNS becomes \"stuck\" in a state",
